 The Triangle Wave Replication Function is a mathematical tool used to create $C^1$-continuous cellular structures. It sets itself apart from the Sawtooth Replication Function by ensuring smooth first-derivative continuity, provided the unit cell geometry is symmetric with respect to planes parallel to the cell faces and passing through their centers. It is chosen when higher continuity is required compared to the basic sawtooth function. The formula is not given in the sources.
 
+### Full Connectivity
+A connectivity criterion for unit cells ensuring that boundary curves at opposite faces are congruent, allowing replicated cells to connect at these curves [S1]. It requires the defining function to be continuous across the entire cellular structure by ensuring function values are identical at opposite faces [S1]. To verify full connectivity, check if $f(x_{min}, y, z) = f(x_{max}, y, z)$ for all $y, z \in I$, $f(x, y_{min}, z) = f(x, y_{max}, z)$ for all $x, z \in I$, and $f(x, y, z_{min}) = f(x, y, z_{max})$ for all $x, y \in I$ [S1]. This ensures $C^0$-continuity of the resulting cellular structure [S1].
